welcome back to 8701  in this chapter we will talk about  neutrinos and we'll start the discussion  with a  relatively simple introduction um how  does the neutrino look in  the standard model and how does it  interact um  we have discussed the neutrino already  quite a bit so this is  more or less a summary in the standard  model the neutrino is massless  it's a massless particle and it acts via  the weak interaction  so it interacts with w bosons and with z  bosons and specifically it does not  interact with photons or blue ones um  you know if you look at the lagrangian  or try to write down the current  uh we find that there is a charge guard  we have a w  and there's a neutral current via the z  boson  it's quite interesting to think about  those two currents a little bit more  so in case of a charged current um  we for example have an incoming neutrino  we can determine  the flavor the kind of neutrino we have  we are detecting the flavor of the  lepton  so if we for example identify an  electron  in this interaction then the initial in  the interaction the initial  neutrino was an electron neutrino while  for the neutral current  when we have um you know some sort of  interaction  happening we cannot identify directly  the neutrino  hence we cannot find the flavor of the  neutrino  you can just measure the sum of all  flavors of neutrinos in the  nutrient count on that story neutrinos  have three flavors they come in  electron flavor muon flavor or tau  flavor  neutrinos are left-handed anti-neutrinos  are right-handed  um so that's the story that's  how neutrinos are characterized in the  standard model  in the standard model we can in the  content  in in the framework we set up um we can  calculate cross-sections scattering  cross-sections  and here we are looking at you know  neutrino nuclear  nuclei scattering again we can split  this up  um in the charged current in neutral car  discussion  but they go very much in in parallel so  we have elastic scattering or in the  case of the charged current we talk  about  quasi-elastic scattering where we have  an incoming neutrino let's say a mu  neutrino  hitting a neutron producing a muon and a  proton it's called quasi elastic because  we do  not break up the target but we change  its  um it's it's kind so we change from a  neutron  to a proton in this case while for the  elastic scattering the neutron just  stays intact  we can also have nuclear resonant  production where we hit the nuclei and  then inside the nuclei  we produce a meson like a  neutral or charged pion and also that  possible in the  in the neutral current exchange and then  we have deep inelastic scattering  uh where we we hit a nuclei  a nucleon that hard that would start  breaking it up  and in this case you know we scatter off  the quark and we produce a new quark  in the charge current interaction in the  same quad  in the neutral carbon interaction um  so you know this is not different from  the stories we had before  the intriguing part about studying  neutrino scattering is that  we do know that we haven't we have  weak interactions being being the  dominant both being the process  while if we um use photons to interact  or we have electrons being intact and we  can have a mixture of weak  and weak and electromagnetic interaction  another important takeaway from this  slide is that when we calculate  cross-section we find that a linear  increase in the cross-section as a  function of the energy  so while cross-sections for neutrino are  small  at higher energy those cross sections  scale linearly  as we can discuss this for neutrino  scattering with  nuclei and nucleons you can also look at  neutrinos scattering with electrons  directly there's a lot of electrons in  the matter around us and when neutrinos  travel through this matter they can  interact with electrons too  and they can leave in the charged  current interaction  a muon and an electron neutrino and a  neutral current interaction  an electron in the mirror nitrogen  also here you see cross-section total  cross-section scale with s with the  energy  good that's the first first introduction  to neutrino physics we'll go into more  detail in the following  presentations

